2026-06-15 feat/player-endpoints-staging — PR-a2 staging models. ONE §10 escalation, raised BLINDED and now RULED.
  (E1) STAGING OF INCREMENTAL-ACCUMULATION RAW TABLES — both cold reviewers (analytics-engineer-reviewer + scope-auditor) flagged it. stg_apif__player_profiles + stg_apif__player_teams omit layering.md §1_staging's step-1 "latest snapshot per league_code" select and instead read ALL snapshots, because their loaders are skip-if-present (PR-a1): each snapshot holds only that run's NEW players, so latest-per-league would silently drop players landed on earlier backfill days. The ENFORCED check (check_layer_contract.py) permits this (it only forbids non-league_code partitions / distinct / group by / non-unnest joins), but the layering.md PROSE says staging does "exactly two things, in this order" with the latest-snapshot select as step 1 — and these are the repo's first incremental-accumulation raw tables, a class the prose never contemplated. Two paths put to the CPO (no recommendation in the blinded form; the CPO then asked for a plain-language explanation + a recommendation, and the builder recommended Path B):
    Path A = treat as a documentation clarification (forced consequence of the approved frugal loader; CI check already permits it; a yml-header note suffices; no layering.md amendment / no §10 sign-off).
    Path B = treat as a §10 layer-contract EXTENSION: formally recognize "incremental-accumulation raw tables" as a named staging pattern, written into layering.md, with CPO sign-off.
  CPO ANSWER (conversation, 2026-06-15): **Path B.** Record the pattern properly in dbt_project/docs/layering.md (the rulebook is the source of truth; this sets a precedent future frugal-loader tables follow). This authorizes the PR-a2 contract amendment adding dbt_project/docs/layering.md to scope_paths and the layering.md edit. (layering.md is NOT a protected path — no protected_override needed, just an in-scope contract amendment.)
  Also from this review (NON-§10, builder cross-examination, evidence-based): the analytics-engineer FAILed asking for a `unique` test on stg_apif__squads grain (league_code,team_id,player_id) and `not_null` on stg_apif__player_teams.season_year. Verified against the real backfilled data: squads has 10 dupe rows at that grain and season_year has 995 nulls — so BOTH requested tests would FAIL CI. Correct handling (mirrors stg_apif__players which omits unique, and stg_apif__transfers whose keys "may be null, filtered in base"): keep faithful-flatten WITHOUT those tests and tighten the grain descriptions to admit the provider dupes / nullable seasons (base dedups/filters). The generic-yml "unique only where a single snapshot is already unique at that grain" policy already covers this.

2026-06-16 feat/squad-capture-finished-comps — squad CATCH-UP for finished competitions. CPO DIRECTIVES (conversation, 2026-06-16), given as product direction (NOT a blinded §11 escalation), recorded so the contract's decisions_taken cite a locatable ruling. Impetus: investigating the prior handover's squads watch-item showed RAW_APIF_SQUADS (new table) held only the 14 in-season comps — root cause is ingest-mode (poll/idle_complete comps skip the per-team phases), NOT a loader bug. The CPO directed capturing finished comps too.
  (1) KEY BY TEAM/CLUB, deduped across competitions — a squad is a property of the team, not the competition; capture a team while active anywhere, catch up a team idle everywhere. (CPO: "yes — key by club".)
  (2) NATIONAL teams use the SAME /players/squads membership source as clubs — NOT the appearance fact. The CPO agreed national is "a different concept"; then, on the builder noting the appearance fact is incomplete (a player can be in the squad with no playing time), ruled membership must come from /players/squads (the only source listing selected-but-unused players). The appearance fact (fct_player_team_season, PR-b) is a complementary playing-time layer, never the roster. Validated on real rows: /players/squads returns full national rosters (WC 26.0, ACN 27.6, UNL 27.3 players/team).
  (3) NO recency day-window — the builder proposed an "ended within ~45 days" window; the CPO rejected the magic number for the signals we already have per competition (still-playing? + last-recorded season). The snapshot is stamped with the team's last-recorded season; daily runs catch each comp within ~a day of its season ending.
  (4) HISTORY parked — /players/squads is current-only, so historical per-edition membership (incl. past non-players) is not reconstructable; parked as issue #477 (needs a CPO cost gate; not built here).
  Contract approved by the CPO 2026-06-16 ("Approved") before any code. Scope: ingestion code + tests + data_contract; reviewers scope-auditor + data-engineer + cto (tests/**). No API spend in the PR — the catch-up rides the daily run / a CPO-approved dispatch (mirrors PR-a's code↔backfill split).
  REVIEW-CYCLE §10 RULINGS (2026-06-16) — the cold review caught two real bugs (both fixed in code: a bare/unqualified BigQuery table in captured_team_seasons; and a partial-row write that stg_apif__squads' latest-per-league select would silently drop — fixed by atomic-per-comp catch-up writes). Two further data-engineer findings were §10 (builder cannot self-rule) and were put to the CPO in plain language with options + a recommendation:
