  - (⭐ the standing rule this produced, and it is general) **"if one comment doesn't pass the PR then you change it and only the new version stays. not the old and the new."** A correction REPLACES; it never accumulates. Applies to code comments, contracts, evidence artifacts and issue bodies. This was the single generator of #370's cost: every round appended "this said X until round N, which was wrong, now Y", keeping both — and that text was itself inside the reviewed diff, so the next round found defects in it. Measured: 134 of 220 added comment lines in `strings.ts` narrated the review rather than explaining the code. Applying the rule took #370's paperwork from ~3.5 MB to ~240 KB and its contract from 643 lines to 171.
  - (banked — WHAT ROUND 1 OF THIS BRANCH PROVED) All three reviewers FAILED it, and **not one finding was about artifact phrasing.** Every one was a real machinery defect: the CI twin still enforcing the old floor, a new code path failing open in the unsafe direction, two stale rule statements in guard files, and two unpinned tests. That is the evidence the change works — the reviewers went back to finding bugs. Keep the diff free of the review's own paperwork and adversarial review pays for itself again.
  - (banked) **The most serious round-1 finding was a verification I claimed and had not done.** `impact_map` said the PASS floor "exists in one place. Verified before editing." It existed in two, and the second was fail-closed CI on every PR, so change 3 was inoperative exactly where it mattered. An `impact_map` asserted from memory is worse than an absent one, because the reviewers are entitled to rely on it. Paste the evidence or do not make the claim.
