2026-08-09 — #39: the nightly LEAVES CI. New mechanism + recurring cost, both APPROVED.

  WHAT PROMPTED IT. `data:nightly` ran the production data plane inside GitLab CI, so a CI-side
  event took the product's data offline twice in four days: the GitHub→GitLab migration silently
  dropped the cron (2026-08-03 → 08-09, six days stale, nothing noticed), then the namespace hit
  `ci_quota_exceeded` and jobs stopped starting. Neither cause had anything to do with data.

  THE MEASUREMENT that settled it (job 15794630814, 2026-08-09): the nightly is ~111 min/run
  against a **400 min/month** free-tier allowance shared across all three of the CPO's projects.
  ~83 of the 105 ingestion minutes are `transfers`, `coaches` and in-season squads re-fetching
  data that has not changed — i.e. #33 item 14. It never fit, from the moment item 7 created the
  schedule. Note the project is already `visibility: public`, and that does NOT exempt it.

  THE CPO RULING that removed the cheap options: "the nightly run is the minimum of updates we
  need so data don't become stale." Cadence is therefore NOT a cost lever. Also ruled out in the
  same conversation: buying GitLab compute minutes, and applying to the GitLab for Open Source
  programme. That leaves hosting as the only variable, which is what #39 changes.

  APPROVED, explicitly and in sequence: "start with #39" → "plan stage 1" → approval of the
  written Stage 1 plan. Region `europe-west1` and Secret Manager for the API key were both
  chosen by the CPO when asked directly.

  THRESHOLDS CROSSED, declared here so they are verifiable rather than asserted in a contract
  that ships with the same commit:
  - NEW MECHANISM — Cloud Run Jobs, Cloud Scheduler and a container image. All new to this repo.
  - RECURRING COST — ~$1.70/month GCP, falling to ~$0.35 after #33 item 14. Against it, the
    whole 400 GitLab minutes/month return to CI across three projects, and today the nightly
    cannot run at all.
  - IAM — TWO new grants to `github-actions-dbt@…`, and it matters that they are enumerated
    because that identity is NOT single-purpose: it is the same account CI impersonates to
    write the prod warehouse. (1) `roles/secretmanager.secretAccessor` on the secret
    `api-football-key`. (2) `roles/run.invoker` on the JOB `fdp-nightly` — RESOURCE-scoped, not
    project-scoped. The project-scoped form would let it invoke every Cloud Run resource in the
    project, present and future, which this task does not need.
    Round 1 caught this: the first draft of the runbook used the project-scoped binding and the
    contract claimed only ONE grant existed. `cto-reviewer` and `platform-reviewer` found it
    independently. Recorded here so a future reader can see the full credential surface of the
    nightly in one place rather than reconstructing it from a runbook.
  No routing row in `.claude/review_routing.json` covers `Dockerfile` or `deploy/**`, so
  `cto-reviewer` was spawned for this task even though routing does not require it. A new
  mechanism with no CTO verdict would be the gap the threshold-declaration rule exists to close.

  DELIBERATELY NOT DONE IN STAGE 1, so the limits are on record rather than discovered later:
  - `.gitlab-ci.yml` is NOT edited. `data:nightly` stays as a manual fallback until Cloud Run has
    proven itself; the GitLab SCHEDULE is paused instead (project config, not a file). Deleting
    the job touches a protected path and needs its own governance task.
  - The staleness blind spot is NOT closed. That is Stage 2 (#33 item 16) and Stage 1 does not
    claim otherwise.
  - The image is not rebuilt on merge. Stage 3. Until then a merge and a redeploy are two acts,
    and between them the nightly runs the previous image.

  SCOPE EXTENSION, asked and granted mid-task: `.gitattributes`.
  The question put to the CPO: may `.gitattributes` be added to scope_paths (two lines — `*.sh`
  and `Dockerfile` pinned to `eol=lf`)? The CPO's answer, verbatim: **"yes, add .gitattributes"**.
  WHY IT WAS ASKED RATHER THAN TAKEN. `core.autocrlf=true` on the dev machine and the repo had
  no `.gitattributes`, so any fresh clone rewrites `deploy/nightly/entrypoint.sh` to CRLF.
  `gcloud run jobs deploy --source .` uploads the WORKING TREE, so the image would ship a
  `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` shebang and the container would die with "no such file or directory"
  — at 04:00, in production. Demonstrated, not theorised: a forced re-checkout produced exactly
  that shebang. It fails nowhere earlier — tests pass, the build succeeds, nothing in CI runs
  the script.
  THE ALTERNATIVE OFFERED and declined: strip the CRs inside the Dockerfile, which is already in
  scope and needs no extension. Declined as symptom-treatment — it only helps files the
  Dockerfile names and leaves the repo corrupting shell scripts for anyone who clones it.
  RECORDED HERE BECAUSE OF A ROUND-2 FAIL. The first fix cited the CPO quote in `contract.md`
  only. `scope-auditor` FAILed that too, correctly: every other CPO decision in this task is
  corroborated in this log, and working_agreement §2 makes the log the only way a reviewer can
  check that a claimed ruling exists. A builder-authored quote inside the artefact whose scope
  it widens is not independent evidence. Two FAILs on the same item, both mine, both right.

  REJECTED, with reasons, so they are not re-proposed: Cloud Composer/Airflow (~$300/month floor
  against a $26/month warehouse); Dataflow or Spark (wrong tool — rate-limited HTTP calls, not
  distributed computation); a self-hosted GitLab runner for the NIGHTLY (removes the bill without
  fixing the coupling, and adds a dependency on a machine being awake at 04:00 — though it
  remains the right answer for human-triggered MR pipelines, which is a separate decision).


2026-08-10 — #39 STAGE 2: A CPO CHOICE THE PLATFORM REFUSED (30h → 25h)

  THE CHOICE. Asked and answered in-thread, alongside the alert channel: the staleness threshold
  is "no successful run in 30 HOURS", picked by the CPO over 26h and 54h. 30h matched the
  `warn_after` already set on 8 sources in sources.yml.

  THE REFUSAL. Cloud Monitoring will not accept it. Both mechanisms were tried against the real
  API and both returned HTTP 400:
    - `conditionAbsent` — "Durations longer than 23h30m are not supported."
    - MQL `absent_for` — "the longest time the query results might be absent is 1d6h, which is
      greater than the allowed 1d1h."
  So the ceiling is 25h, and 23h30m is not a fallback: for a DAILY job consecutive successes are
  ~24h apart, so a 23h30m window fires shortly before every single run. Anything at or below 24h
  is a daily false alarm. 25h is the ONLY value above 24h either mechanism permits, which is why
  the staleness policy is MQL rather than the simpler `conditionAbsent`.

  WHAT THE BUILDER DID WRONG, first: substituted 25h, DEPLOYED it, and wrote up the reasoning.
  `scope-auditor` FAILed that in round 1 and was right. §10 makes a threshold a CPO decision
  "regardless of how obvious the answer seems", and §11 requires the conflicting paths and a
  recommendation put to the CPO BEFORE implementing. Documenting an override after shipping is
  not holding authority for it. A constraint that leaves exactly one viable NUMBER does not make
  the DECISION unavailable — the real fork was never "which number", it was "accept a tighter
  alert / drop staleness detection until item 14 / measure something else entirely", and only
  the CPO could weigh those.

  THE ESCALATION, run properly the second time, with three paths and a recommendation:
    A. keep 25h — fastest detection the platform allows, ~1h of drift tolerance (RECOMMENDED)
    B. disable staleness until item 14 — no false alarms, blind spot stays open
    C. build a BigQuery-backed check — no cap, measures data age rather than job success,
       but a bigger build than Stage 2 as scoped
  CPO ANSWER: **"Build a BigQuery-backed check instead."** — path C, against the builder's
  recommendation.

  WHY C IS BETTER, now that it is built. Job success was always a PROXY. A pipeline that
  "succeeds" every night while writing nothing is stale, and no absence-of-success alert would
  ever have caught it. `scripts/check_raw_freshness.py` measures table age directly, so the 25h
  ceiling stops applying entirely, and the thresholds come from `sources.yml` — the same ones
  `dbt source freshness` uses, so there is no second copy to drift. Running it HOURLY also makes
  absence detection cheap: a 3h window, far inside the 23h30m cap that made the original design
  awkward. Cost is zero — `get_table()` is a metadata call, not a query.

  A CONSEQUENCE WORTH KEEPING: because the sentinel covers "succeeding but stale" out of band,
  the in-entrypoint `dbt source freshness` added earlier in this task was REVERTED. It had
  forced `dbt deps` above the new_data gate and turned a quiet night from $0 into a few cents.
  The sentinel does the same job better, for free, without touching the nightly at all.

  A SECOND API CONSTRAINT, same class, recorded so it is not rediscovered: one policy cannot
  hold both the staleness and the failure condition — "Alert policies with
  monitoring_query_language condition type can only have a single condition" (HTTP 400). Shipped
  as TWO policies sharing one notification channel.

  NOT YET PROVEN, stated plainly: the Monitoring API returns no `verificationStatus` field for
  the email channel, so whether delivery works is UNKNOWN from this side. An unverified channel
  is a policy that looks armed and is not. Stage 2 is not "done" until an email has actually
  arrived — a policy that has never fired is decoration, the same as a test that has never gone
  red.


2026-08-10 — #33 ITEM 14: THE 7-DAY RE-FETCH CADENCE, AND TWO CONSEQUENCES THE BUILDER DERIVED

  WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS AT ALL. `scope-auditor` FAILed the task for recording every one of these
  decisions in `contract.md` only. It was right, and the precedent it cited is the entry above
  from 2026-08-09, where the identical pattern was FAILed twice on the `.gitattributes`
  amendment: "a builder-authored quote inside the artefact whose scope it widens is not
  independent evidence". THIRD OCCURRENCE OF THE SAME MISS, by the same builder, two days after
  writing that sentence. The lesson is not "remember harder": the moment a CPO answers anything,
  it belongs here BEFORE the contract cites it.

  THE RULING, verbatim, in-thread: **"re-fetch transfers and coaches every 7 days"**.
  Context put to the CPO first: `transfers` 26.7 min + `coaches` 18.7 min = 45 of the 105
  ingestion minutes measured on the 2026-08-09 nightly, spent re-downloading unchanged data,
  plus ~3,800 of ~8,300 daily API calls. The CPO framed it as "we had a setup that ensures not
  fetching data we already have... fix it".

  A PREMISE CHECK RUN BEFORE ACCEPTING THAT FRAMING, recorded because it changes what the task
  is. `git log -S` across ALL history for `already_captured`, `captured_`, `skip_if_present`,
  `cached_response` and `players_needing`, scoped to `loads/transfers.py` and
  `loads/coaches.py`, returns ZERO commits. Neither loader has EVER had skip-if-present logic,
  so nothing regressed and nothing was "changed to the worse". The CPO's recollection of the
  DESIGN is nonetheless correct: it exists deliberately in five other loaders (`players`,
  `player_squads`, `player_profiles`, `player_teams`, `fixtures`, `fixture_details`). This task
  finishes an unfinished job rather than restoring a broken one.

  TWO CONSEQUENCES THE BUILDER DERIVED AND PUT IN THE PLAN THE CPO APPROVED. Both are flagged
  here as builder-derived rather than ruled, because neither follows from a literal reading of
  "every 7 days":

  1. THE FRESHNESS THRESHOLDS MOVE WITH THE CADENCE — and this LOOSENS A GUARD.
     `sources.yml` had warn 30h / error 54h on both tables. Under a 7-day cadence they sit
     permanently past `error_after`. Raised to warn 8d / error 10d for these two tables only.
     THE COST, stated plainly: genuine breakage of transfers or coaches ingestion is now
     invisible for up to 10 days instead of 54 hours.
     WHY IT IS STILL THE NARROW FIX and not a blanket widening: 54h is simply FALSE once the
     intended refresh interval is 7 days, and a guard that fires daily on correct behaviour gets
     muted — which is a worse outcome than a wider true one. The other nine sources are
     untouched, and a test pins `error_after > cadence + one cycle` so the two cannot drift.
     WHEN IT BITES: nothing on the item-14 branch reads these thresholds (`dbt source freshness`
     is invoked nowhere). The consumer is the hourly sentinel on the SEPARATE, unmerged #39
     Stage 2 branch (!30, commit 42ba08f). Until that merges this is inert metadata.

  2. THE STAGGER, `md5(league_code) % 7`, is a builder addition.
     Without it every league shares tonight's `ingested_at` and all 45 come due on the SAME
     night — one night in seven costing the full 45 minutes against a 3h timeout, six costing
     nothing. Staggering spreads it to ~6-7 leagues a night.
     ⚠ THE FIRST IMPLEMENTATION BROKE THE CPO'S RULING and a reviewer caught it: it computed
     `due_after = interval - offset` on EVERY call, which permanently SHORTENED the interval, so
     a league with offset 6 re-fetched every single DAY and only offset 0 ever got 7 days. The
     stagger must shift WHICH day a league runs, never how often. Now a calendar slot
     (`(ordinal + offset) % interval == 0`) with a safety net at `age >= interval`.

  SCOPE AMENDMENT: `ingestion/api_football/orchestrator.py`, authority = the CPO-approved plan,
  which specifies the read is "called ONCE per table per run and passed down, mirroring
  `captured_player_team_seasons` (`orchestrator.py:201`)". The hoist can only live where the
  per-competition loop is, so the approved design already required this file; its omission from
  `scope_paths` was clerical. The contract gate caught it on the first edit.

  ALSO CORRECTED, from a reviewer finding: four places in this task asserted that
  `RAW_APIF_COACHES` is merge-on-write since 8b. It is APPEND-ONLY and was explicitly EXCLUDED
  from 8b by the ruling of 2026-06-23 recorded above, because `stg_apif__coaches` reads ALL
  snapshots to preserve every coach ever seen. The builder wrote that exclusion two days ago and
  then contradicted it. The skip design is unaffected — skipping writes nothing either way —
  but the stated reason was wrong for one of the two tables the task is about.


2026-08-11 — #53: APPLY THE fixture_event_team_overrides ROW FOR MACAU (1544) / MAÇÃO (4767)

  RECORDED HERE BEFORE THE CONTRACT CITES IT, which is the whole point of this entry existing
  as its own step. Three tasks in the last two days FAILed review for recording a CPO decision
  only inside `contract.md` — the artefact whose own scope the decision authorises. The ruling
  is also published on GitLab #53 as a comment timestamped 2026-08-11T12:02:55Z, i.e. BEFORE
  the branch existed, so a blinded reviewer can verify it independently of anything the builder
  wrote.

  THE RULING, verbatim, in-thread: **"add the seed row"** — in answer to the recommendation on
  #53 to apply `4767 -> 1544` with mode `reattribute_if_cohabiting`, NOT `alias`.

  WHAT WAS PUT TO THE CPO BEFORE THE ANSWER, so the ruling is on evidence and not on the
  builder's inference. The prod nightly of 2026-08-11 failed:
  `assert_event_team_in_fixture_participants` returned 10 rows, `dbt build` halted, and 540 of
  970 nodes SKIPped — so ingestion kept succeeding while `dbt_analytics` went stale, and it
  recurs every night until fixed. All 10 events sit in two WCQAS fixtures (1100381, 1100382,
  the same two nations home and away) and every one names team 4767.

  THE EXTERNAL GROUND TRUTH, which this repo's standing rule requires before asserting that two
  entity records relate (`feedback_verify_real_world_identity`): **Mação is a municipality in
  Portugal; Macau is an AFC member association.** A Portuguese municipality cannot appear in the
  Asian section of World Cup qualifying. Both facts are externally checkable and were stated to
  the CPO on #53 before the ruling.

  THE INTERNAL EVIDENCE, re-verified against PROD on 2026-08-11 by this task rather than taken
  from the issue text: Mação (4767) is a participant in **0** fixtures warehouse-wide and its
  only events anywhere are these 10; Macau (1544) is a participant in exactly the 2 fixtures
  whose events are mis-tagged. 4767 exists in `dim_team` only as an attribution artefact.

  WHY `reattribute_if_cohabiting` AND NOT `alias`, since the two modes are not interchangeable
  and the wrong one fails in opposite directions. `alias` is an UNCONDITIONAL id replacement and
  means "one club, two provider ids" — it would permanently conflate a Portuguese municipality
  with a national team, so if Mação ever legitimately appeared in Portuguese football its events
  would be silently folded into Macau. `reattribute_if_cohabiting` fires only where the correct
  id IS a fixture participant and the wrong id is NOT, which is exactly the ASKO Kara precedent
  already in this seed (#526). The failure modes are asymmetric: `alias` is silently wrong
  forever, `reattribute_if_cohabiting` is at worst inert.

  THE CPO'S OWN VERIFICATION CONDITION, quoted because it is a done_when and not advice:
  "verify by running its SQL directly (it is in `dbt_project/tests/`), NOT by assuming the seed
  took effect. The `reattribute_if_cohabiting` mode is conditional, so a row that does not match
  its condition silently does nothing." The seed landing in git is NOT evidence the test passes.

  ONE IMPLEMENTATION DETAIL THE BUILDER DECIDED, declared because it changes the bytes of the
  CPO-specified row: the note text contains a comma ("Portuguese municipality, name collision"),
  and this is a 4-column CSV. Written unquoted it parses as 5 fields and the seed breaks (counted
  with `csv.reader`, not eyeballed — an earlier draft of this entry said 6). The
  note is therefore wrapped in double quotes — standard RFC 4180 quoting, already used in
  `team_name_overrides.csv` and `metric_catalogue.csv`. The VALUE is byte-identical to the
  CPO's; only the CSV framing differs. Not a decision about content.

  NOT DECIDED HERE, and deliberately not touched: whether `dim_team` should stop publishing
  4767 at all (it is a real Portuguese entity that our data simply never sees play), and whether
  the provider should be asked to correct the feed. Both are separate from making the nightly
  green.


2026-08-12 fix/61-alert-policy-apply-loop — #61: THE RULING IS "GO AHEAD", AND IT COVERS LESS
  THAN THE FIRST DRAFT OF THE CONTRACT CLAIMED

  WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS. `scope-auditor` FAILed round 1 because `contract.md` asserted the CPO
  "approved in-thread ('go ahead') after being shown the three proposed fixes" and no matching
  record existed anywhere. It was right on both counts, and the second is the worse one: the
  sequence was BACKWARDS. The CPO said "go ahead" to a one-line mention of the issue; the
  builder described the three-part plan AFTERWARDS and then built it. Writing that up as a
  ruling given on a plan the CPO had seen is the Appendix A2 pattern — a decision presented as
  agreed. Corrected before commit.

  THE RULING, verbatim: **"go ahead"** — in answer to "Next up when you want to continue, in
  order: the alert-deploy bug I found, then the squads issue, then making staging a table."
  So the authority is: START #61 AS FILED. Nothing narrower, nothing wider.

  WHAT #61 AS FILED CONTAINS, since that is now the whole boundary. It was written by the
  builder on 2026-08-11 and lists three suggested fixes: (1) derive the apply loop from the
  file instead of `for i in 0 1`, (2) correct the prose that says the file "holds BOTH
  policies", (3) "Consider making the apply step genuinely idempotent".

  ITEMS 1 AND 2 ARE THE BUG and are covered outright.

  ITEM 3 IS A BUILDER DECISION and is labelled as one in the contract rather than attributed to
  the CPO. Reasoning, recorded so it can be attacked: the README currently DOCUMENTS the hazard
  instead of removing it — it warns that re-running duplicates every policy and that duplicates
  are "how people learn to ignore alerts". A recipe that must be run carefully is the same
  class of defect as a count that must be updated carefully, and that class already failed in
  production. It crosses no §10 line: no new mechanism, no recurring cost, no product, naming
  or permanent decision. It is separable — item 3 can be reverted without touching 1 and 2.

  EXPLICITLY NOT TAKEN, and reserved to the CPO: turning the apply step into a real script
  (`scripts/apply_alert_policies.py` or similar). That WOULD be a new operational mechanism. The
  builder wrote exactly that into the README on the first attempt, contradicting its own
  contract, and reverted it before review. The recipe stays an inline documented block.

  WHAT THE BUG ACTUALLY WAS, for whoever reads this later: `deploy/nightly/README.md` applied
  the Cloud Monitoring policies with `for i in 0 1` over a generator that writes one payload per
  policy declared in `deploy/nightly/alert-policy.json`. The file grew to three. Index 2 was
  generated and never sent, so `fdp freshness sentinel itself stopped` — the policy that fires
  when the freshness sentinel itself dies — was never deployed. It FAILS OPEN: the dropped alert
  is the one whose absence nothing else can reveal. Found 2026-08-11 while deleting the
  superseded 25h bridge policy, and the two together would have left "nightly and sentinel both
  dead" with no alert at all. The instance was fixed by hand that day
  (`alertPolicies/751471063453009885`); this task fixes the RECIPE.

  A SECOND REVIEWER FINDING WORTH KEEPING, because the guard repeated the defect it polices.
  `platform-reviewer` FAILed round 1: the first version of `tests/test_alert_policy_recipe.py`
  matched only the SHAPE OF THE RETIRED BASH RECIPE (`for i in 0 1`, `{0..1}`, `polN.json`).
  The fixed recipe is Python, so all three patterns were already unreachable — `["policies"][:2]`,
  an `islice(..., 2)` or a `break` would have dropped the third policy with every test green.
  A guard written against one dead idiom is a literal standing in for the real rule, which is
  the same defect one level up. Replaced with a STRUCTURAL check: the recipe's Python block is
  parsed with `ast`, the loop over the declared policies is located, and its iterable must
  contain no number, no slice, no `islice` and no `break`. Both Python-shaped regressions were
  then verified to go red.

  ROUND 2 THEN FAILED THAT TOO, correctly, with four rewrites that reproduce #61 while passing
  every assertion: hoisting the slice into a variable whose name contains "policies", a
  `continue` on one displayName, a filter baked into a generator expression, and a post-loop
  DELETE. The AST check only ever inspected the loop's iterable expression and the body's
  `break`. The docstring also claimed the check "holds for any rewording", which was simply
  false. THE REAL LESSON, third time in one task: a static check tests how code LOOKS and the
  defect is about what it DOES. Replaced by EXECUTING the recipe against a stubbed Monitoring
  API and asserting every declared policy receives a write. All four rewrites verified red.

  ROUND 3 CAP-OVERRIDE — CPO ASKED, ANSWERED, RECORDED BEFORE `review.md` CITES IT.
  Round 3 FAILed with a finding sharper than either earlier one, and it is worth keeping
  whatever happens to this branch: an executed test that compares the recipe against TODAY'S
  `alert-policy.json` cannot distinguish "iterates every policy" from "hardcoded to a bound
  that happens to equal today's count". `[:3]` against a 3-policy file passes all 8 tests —
  and that is #61's exact mechanism, because `for i in 0 1` was ALSO correct on the day it was
  written, when there were two policies. The bug is invisible until the next policy is added,
  which is the moment it does its damage.

  `docs/working_agreement.md` §2 caps the review loop at 3 rounds and requires the builder to
  STOP and bring open findings to the CPO rather than grind a fourth. Put to the CPO with three
  paths: (A) one more round applying the fix, (B) ship the recipe fix alone and file the test
  separately, (C) park the branch.
  CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-08-12): **"One more round"** — path A, the builder's
  recommendation. `review.md` therefore carries `rounds: 4` with a `rounds_cap_override`.

  THE FIX THE OVERRIDE BUYS: run the recipe against a SYNTHETIC policy set whose size differs
  from the real file, so any count baked into the recipe — a slice, a literal name list, an
  index bound — fails the moment it is written rather than the next time a policy is added.
  Count-independence is the property the deleted AST check had and the executed test lacked;
  this gets it without going back to inspecting source.

  ROUND 4 THEN FOUND THE FIX WAS ASYMMETRIC, and this one is subtle enough to be worth writing
  down. Both new synthetic tests passed `existing=[]`, so every synthetic policy took the
  CREATE branch. NOTHING in the file drove the UPDATE/PATCH branch at any count other than the
  real file's three. A bound placed inside that branch —

      if name in existing:
          if patch_count >= 3: continue

  — passes all ten tests: the real-file update test has exactly three pre-existing policies so
  the counter runs out exactly as the loop ends, and the synthetic tests never enter the branch
  at all. It then stops reconciling the fourth policy forever, in the ordinary steady state
  where policies already exist. Same #61 shape, relocated one branch over. Count-independence
  has to hold on BOTH branches or it is not count-independence.

  ROUND 5 AUTHORISED. Round 4 was itself an override, so a fifth needed its own decision. Put
  to the CPO as part of a sequencing plan that named it "one small test fix and a final review
  round".
  CPO ANSWER (in-thread, 2026-08-12): **"go"**.
  `review.md` carries `rounds: 5` with the `rounds_cap_override` naming both grants.

  THE FIX: synthetic-set tests that drive the UPDATE branch (all pre-existing) and a MIXED set
  (some existing, some new — the real steady state after a policy is added), asserting every
  declared policy is reconciled on every branch regardless of set size.

  ROUND 5: scope-auditor PASS. platform-reviewer FAIL with two more, both real.
  (a) EVERY "larger than today" fixture used exactly 5, so a bound of 5 — the size the test
      suite itself standardised on — was invisible to all twelve tests. The hardcoded number
      had simply MOVED from the recipe (3, today's real count) into the test (5, today's
      chosen stress size). Fixed by parametrising over several sizes.
  (b) MORE SERIOUS, and the best finding of the five rounds: no test ever inspected the URL of
      a PATCH, only its body. A recipe that derived the target resource id wrongly — say
      `list(existing.values())[0]["name"]` instead of `existing[name]["name"]` — sends one
      PATCH per policy, each carrying its own correct displayName, so every test passes while
      in production every PATCH lands on the SAME alert policy. One real policy is overwritten
      repeatedly and all the others silently go stale, while the recipe prints "updated" for
      each. That is WORSE than #61: #61 left one policy undeployed, this leaves N-1 permanently
      unreconciled. Fixed by asserting each PATCH URL carries the id of the policy named in its
      own body.

  ROUND 6 AUTHORISED, AND SCOPED. Third time past the cap. Put to the CPO with the honest
  framing that the RECIPE fix is reviewed and solid and it is the TEST that keeps failing, and
  that the marginal value was falling while the cost was not.
  CPO ANSWER (AskUserQuestion, 2026-08-12): **"Fix both, one scoped round, then stop"**.
  The round is a DELTA judging whether those two findings are closed — which is what
  `docs/working_agreement.md` §2 already defines a re-review to be ("told only what changed
  since its own last PASS and judges that plus its prior findings"), not a lowered bar. If it
  does not pass, the builder STOPS and the residue is filed as its own issue rather than
  ground for a seventh round.

