2026-08-12 — #33 ITEMS 9 + 10 (STAGING AS A TABLE) AND ITEM 15 (DROP /INJURIES)

  THE RULINGS. Item 9 + 10: **"definitely"**. Item 15: **"yes"**. Both in-thread, 2026-08-12,
  after the analysis below was put to the CPO. Recorded before any contract cites them.

  ⚠ A PREMISE CHECK THE CPO DEMANDED, AND IT MATTERED. Asked directly: "it was a table before
  then we made it a view, for cost reasons. are you sure?" If true, this task would be reversing
  a measured decision and would need to explain what was wrong with that measurement.

  IT IS NOT TRUE, and the evidence is mechanical rather than recollected: `1_staging` has been
  `+materialized: view` at EVERY commit that ever touched `dbt_project/dbt_project.yml`, from
  `1f422c0` (2026-04-10, the file's creation) through `4899025` (2026-08-02). Extracted by
  replaying the 1_staging block at each commit, not by reading the current comment. Staging has
  never been a table and was never switched to a view for cost reasons.

  WHAT THE CPO WAS REMEMBERING IS `2_base`, which genuinely WAS a view and became a table on
  2026-08-02 under #547 — and there the belief "not storing an intermediate result is cheaper"
  is exactly what the measurement overturned. So the recollection was real, applied to the
  neighbouring layer, and pointed at the correct precedent for THIS change rather than against
  it.

  THE ECONOMICS ARE SMALLER THAN #33 CLAIMS, AND THE CONTRACT SAYS SO RATHER THAN QUOTING THE
  ISSUE. #33 justified item 9 on `RAW_APIF_TRANSFERS` at 6.99 GiB being re-scanned per test.
  Item 8b (merge-on-write) has since shrunk that table to **0.178 GiB, a 39x reduction**, so
  the single largest scan the item was written about is already gone. Measured on the last 24h
  of prod builds: tests $0.17 vs models $0.07 — the same 2.4x view-rescan signature, but on a
  much smaller base. Expected saving is roughly **$3-4/month**, not the figure #33 implies.
  ⚠ Do not quote #33's transfers numbers in any future contract; they are stale.

  WHY IT IS STILL RIGHT, on the argument that survives the smaller number: each raw table is
  currently scanned about five times a night per staging model — once per test plus once per
  base read — because a view stores nothing and every reader re-executes the JSON parse. As a
  table it is scanned ONCE and the 59 staging tests read a stored table at BigQuery's 10 MB
  minimum. What the change removes is the MULTIPLIER, which applies to whatever raw grows to.
  The CPO stated in the same thread that more competitions ARE coming, which is precisely the
  condition that makes the multiplier the thing worth removing. Storage added is ~0.5 GiB
  (measured: the whole `dbt_analytics` base layer, which is staging's parsed output, is
  0.415 GiB across 43 tables), i.e. cents per month.

  ITEM 15 IS THE BIGGER AND SIMPLER WIN, and it surfaced from the same measurement rather than
  from the issue text: `RAW_APIF_INJURIES` is now the **LARGEST raw table at 1.975 GiB** — the
  merge-on-write work reordered the table, it used to be second — and NOTHING reads it. No
  source declaration, no model, no script. Pure storage cost with no consumer and no downstream
  to reason about.

  SEQUENCED AS TWO TASKS at the CPO's agreement, not bundled: items 9+10 change a layer's
  materialisation policy and its CI guard; item 15 stops an ingest and deletes a raw table.
  Different blast radii, different reviewers, and bundling them would make either hard to
  revert alone.

  ⚠ THE `.claude/active_work.md` OWNERSHIP RULING, WRITTEN DOWN HERE BECAUSE A REVIEWER COULD
  NOT VERIFY IT AND WAS RIGHT TO FAIL THE TASK OVER IT.

  THE RULING, verbatim, from the GitLab #33 issue body and repeated in the CPO's session brief:
  **"Do not edit `.claude/active_work.md` from this worktree — the product stream owns it.
  Record state here instead."** #33 also states the split it rests on: "Worktree:
  `D:\Projects\fdp-pipeline`. The product stream runs in parallel in `D:\Projects\fdp-product`."

  WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS. `scope-auditor` FAILed round 2 of items 9/10 because the contract
  justified excluding that file from a drift guard by citing "#33 says so" — an authority
  living on a GitLab issue, which a blinded reviewer cannot read. It called the citation
  unverifiable and noted that `CLAUDE.md`'s memory index says the opposite ("Authoritative
  handover = the in-repo `.claude/active_work.md` ... Read it first, every session"), with no
  evidence anywhere in the repo of a two-stream split. Every one of those observations is
  correct. The FACT was true and the RECORD was not, which is the same defect this log has now
  caught four separate times: an authority the builder can see and the reviewer cannot is not
  an authority.

  WHAT THE RULING DOES AND DOES NOT LICENCE. It forbids EDITING the file from this worktree. It
  does NOT licence silently narrowing a guard that catches a real defect in it. The reviewer's
  characterisation of the first attempt — "a genuine drift-detector was made not to see the
  file it just caught something in" — was accurate, and the exclusion is only defensible now
  because (a) the authority is recorded here where a reviewer can check it, (b) the staleness is
  TRACKED rather than swallowed: `.claude/active_work.md:184` says "staging is still a VIEW so
  tests re-scan raw", which this branch makes false, and it is reported to the owning stream on
  #33 in the same session, and (c) the file is transient handover state that its own header
  calls "CURRENT STATE ONLY", not a policy site any consumer of `layering.md` relies on.

  THE ALTERNATIVE THE REVIEWER OFFERED — add the file to `scope_paths` and fix the one word
  under the 2026-08-08 standing rule, as was done for the two ingestion comments in this same
  diff — IS NOT AVAILABLE HERE, and that is the only reason it was not taken: the standing rule
  permits fixing a broken reference, while this ruling separately forbids touching this
  particular file from this worktree. Where the two conflict, the specific, later, file-scoped
  instruction governs. If the CPO would rather it be corrected in place, that is one word and
  this entry is the place to overturn it.


