  or re-hosting their images at all decides which of the three options is even available.

- (⭐ NOT AN ESCALATION — A RULING I FAILED TO FIND, recorded so the next person finds it)
  `data:build:mr` failed on this branch with
  `Database Error in test assert_metric_catalogue_expr_resolvable / Unrecognized name:
  computation_kind`. This PR adds the `computation_kind` COLUMN to `metric_catalogue.csv` and, in
  the same PR, tightens the guard that reads it. The deferred singular-test step resolves
  `ref('metric_catalogue')` to MAIN's seed, which has no such column yet.

  ⛔ THE REPO HAD ALREADY RULED ON THIS, TWICE, and both rulings are written next to the code. See
  the CI notes in `dbt_project/tests/assert_metric_meaning_complete.sql` and
  `assert_metric_direction_lower_is_better_agree.sql`: *"a change to catalogue VALUES and a guard
  that depends on those values cannot land in the same PR — the values merge first, then the guard.
  Do not try to solve this with a CI workflow change."* Both of those guards shipped that way. This
  is the THIRD instance of the same sequence.

  APPLIED: `assert_metric_catalogue_expr_resolvable.sql` is reverted to main's version here. The
  column still ships and is still covered — by its `accepted_values` SCHEMA test, which runs inside
  `dbt build --select state:modified+` against `ci_analytics` and passed there. The tightened guard
  follows in its own MR once the column is in prod.

  ⚠ WHAT I DID FIRST, AND WHY IT WAS WRONG. I diagnosed this as a structural hole ("the DQ gate
  cannot see an MR's own seed columns"), got CPO approval for a one-word CI change, and opened a
  governance branch with `protected_override` to drop `--favor-state` from the DQ-test step. Both
  routed reviewers FAILED it:
  · `cto-reviewer` — my premise was false. The build step DOES validate the branch's own seed; the
    log shows `10 of 41 PASS assert_metric_catalogue_expr_resolvable` against `ci_analytics`. The DQ
    step merely re-tests it against prod. Nothing shipped unverified.
  · `platform-reviewer` — the change would have created a FALSE GREEN. `ci_analytics` is shared
    across every MR and never cleaned, so an untouched model could be tested against another
    branch's stale leftover and pass while prod is broken. My contract claimed "never a false
    green" categorically; that was asserted, not demonstrated, and it is wrong.
  · It also found the two rulings above, which I had missed.

  THE REUSABLE LESSON, and it is the one this repo logs most often (#904): my search for prior
  guidance covered `docs/` only, while the ruling lived in `dbt_project/tests/`. **A claim of
  absence must search as wide as the claim.** The branch is deleted and nothing was pushed.


2026-08-14 — THE COMPLETENESS GATE AND THE RE-FETCH CADENCE CONTRADICT EACH OTHER (OUR OWN BUG)

  THE RULING: **"Do as recommended"** — option 1, teach the completeness gate that a deliberate
  skip is not a gap. Given in-thread 2026-08-14 after the choice was put in plain language, with
  a recommendation and the reason for rejecting the alternative.

  WHAT BROKE, and it is ours. The 2026-08-14 04:00 nightly exited 3 during ingestion:
  `Completeness gate failed: per-team gaps not healing: UCL/TRANSFERS (1 then 1 teams missing)`.
  `detect_stagnant_per_team_gaps` fails a run when a (league, entity) pair has missing teams on
  TWO CONSECUTIVE RUNS, on the explicit reasoning that one bad run self-heals on the next night's
  fetch. **#33 item 14 (!31, merged 2026-08-11) invalidated that** by putting transfers on a
  7-day per-league cadence that skips the phase ENTIRELY. A skipped league cannot heal — there is
  no fetch — so a pre-existing 1-team gap now trips a gate built for a nightly fetch. The data is
  fine; the question stopped making sense.

  THE TWO PATHS PUT TO THE CPO:
    1. Make the gate skip-aware: do not call a pair stagnant if it was deliberately skipped.
       RECOMMENDED. Keeps the coverage, fixes the wrong assumption.
    2. Drop TRANSFERS from `PER_TEAM_GATED`, matching the existing COACHES precedent. Smaller,
       and it works, but it permanently removes real coverage to work around an assumption WE
       broke — the "never loosen a guard, narrow it to where it still holds" rule.
  CPO ANSWER: option 1.

  ⚠ A NEAR-MISS WORTH RECORDING. `COACHES` is in `PER_TEAM_ENTITIES` but deliberately NOT in
  `PER_TEAM_GATED`, excluded on 2026-08-03 because ~23 of 1,265 teams genuinely have no coach and
  "a permanently-red gate trains everyone to ignore the alarm". Item 14 put coaches AND transfers
  on the same cadence. Only transfers gates, so only transfers broke. The exclusion that saved
  coaches was written for an unrelated reason and covered this one by luck, not by design.

  THE GENERAL LESSON, which is the reason this entry is long: item 14 was reviewed by four
  reviewers across several rounds and none of us connected a change in FETCH CADENCE to a gate
  whose logic assumes NIGHTLY FETCHES. The two mechanisms live in different files, and the
  contract's impact_map traced the dbt lineage of the tables involved but not the OPERATIONAL
  checks that read the same tables. An impact map that stops at the data lineage misses gates.

  STILL OPEN, and reserved to the CPO rather than decided here: whether a league skipped for many
  consecutive runs should eventually be force-fetched, so a real gap cannot hide behind the
  cadence. The 7-day bound limits the exposure, which is why it is not urgent.


2026-08-14 feat/62-registry-seed-display-fields - CPO rulings on how the taxonomy is defined.

  THE ASK. #62 step 1 projects the registry's display fields into the seed so no page has to read
  an authoring file. The first design carried `country` across and used
  `competition_types.single_country` to tell "England" apart from "Europe".

  RULING 1 - THE RULE GOVERNS DATA, NOT EVERYTHING ON A PAGE. CPO: "if the content is related to
  data we want to show... but there might be content where we don't pull from a mart." Correct, and
  #62's wording overreached. Recorded as a note on #62: data comes from exactly one mart per block;
  copy comes from the i18n surface; where data needs a label the mart carries the KEY and the
  English name, never the translated string. `content_architecture.md` principle 4 already said the
  first half; #57's `label_en` + `label_i18n_key` already implemented the third.

  RULING 2 - WE DEFINE THE TAXONOMY; WE DO NOT INHERIT A MESS. CPO: "Our transformation layer is
  the place where we clean, reconcile and standardize the data. We can even impute missing
  information after researching properly... We don't need to accept this. Raw doesn't define the
  taxonomies or categories or whatever. We do it in a way that makes sense for our purpose."

  RULING 3 - AND A FLAG IS NOT A MODEL. On the proposal to split country from continent using
  `single_country`, the CPO: "separating these entries with THIS flag is bullshit... I'd assume
  that these entries are somewhere in a mapping table and/or then a dim table if we use them for a
  mart."

  He was right, and checking proved the proposal worse than it looked. `dim_league` ALREADY carries
  `league_country` AND `country_flag_url`, provider-sourced via `stg_apif__leagues.sql:53`. The
  registry's hand-typed `country` is a SECOND copy; projecting it would have made a THIRD, and the
  flag rule would have encoded the inconsistency instead of removing it. There is no countries seed
  and no `dim_country` anywhere, and `league_country` has no tests at all (`core.yml`: tests: None).

  WHAT CHANGED AS A RESULT. `country` is dropped from this task's projection entirely - the seed
  gets `confederation`, `slug`, `sort_order`, `tier`, `season_type` and nothing else. Countries
  become their own entity under GitLab #69: a `countries` seed and `dim_country` on the
  `confederations` pattern, with FKs from the four dims that hold country as free text, so "Europe"
  and "International" fail a foreign key rather than needing a rule to catch them. First action
  there is DISCOVERY (a priced `SELECT DISTINCT` per surface), then the canonical names go to the
  CPO, because names are copy and copy is his.

  ⚠ NOT DECIDED HERE, and left to #69 deliberately: what happens to the registry YAML's own
  `country` values. Blanking the 24 non-country entries today would change what the site renders,
  because `export_site_data.py:911` still reads the YAML directly - the coupling #62 exists to
  remove. It follows step 4, not this step.


2026-08-15 fix/62-standardize-country-names - CPO rulings on country names, and one of mine
  withdrawn.

  ⚠ FIRST, A CORRECTION TO THE ENTRY ABOVE. Ruling 3's supporting paragraph states `dim_league`
  ALREADY carries `league_country` AND `country_flag_url`, provider-sourced. That was true of the
  COLUMNS and false of the VALUES: all 45 rows were NULL, because `stg_apif__leagues.sql` read
  `$.league.country` and `$.league.flag`, paths that do not exist in the payload - the provider
  puts country as a SIBLING of `league`. `!43` repointed both paths, so the sentence is true NOW.
  Left standing rather than edited, because this log records what was said when it was said; the
  correction belongs here, appended. Ruling 3's CONCLUSION is unaffected and in fact strengthened -
  a real provider source makes the registry's hand-typed copy more redundant, not less.

  THE ASK, and it should not have been one. Having fixed the path, I found the provider's strings
  were not display-ready: `Saudi-Arabia`, `South-Korea`, `USA`. I put those three to the CPO as a
  copy choice under §10 and marked #62 step 3 blocked on his answer.

  RULING 1 - THAT WAS NOT A QUESTION. CPO: "As said earlier, we standardize in base." Ruling 2 of
  2026-08-14 already covered it. Standardising a provider's spelling is the transformation layer's
  job; it is not copy, and asking which spelling to render was asking him to adjudicate what a rule
  settles. The line: the transformation layer decides the FORM, the CPO decides the NAME. Only a
  genuine naming choice crosses into §10.

  RULING 2 - THE ONE PART THAT WAS HIS. CPO: "United States of America", verbatim, chosen over the
  shorter "United States" I had proposed. Recorded in the seed row's `source` column, because that
  is a naming choice and naming choices are his.

  WHAT CHANGED AS A RESULT. `country_name_overrides.csv` + a left join and `coalesce` in
  `base_apif__leagues` + `assert_country_name_overrides_still_needed`. Three parts, all copied from
  `team_name_overrides`; no new mechanism. `dim_league` unchanged - base corrects, the dim
  publishes. Shipped as `!45`.

  ⚠ NOT a hyphen-to-space rule, and this is the part worth remembering. It looks like the
  class-level fix and is wrong: `Guinea-Bissau` and `Timor-Leste` are correctly hyphenated country
  names, so a blanket replace corrupts them the moment either is onboarded. "Fix the class, not the
  instance" does NOT mean reach for the pattern - it means find the mechanism that cannot be holed.
  Here the explicit mapping is safe and the regex is not, and the real class-level fix is #69's
  foreign key, where an unknown country fails rather than renders.

  ⚠ NOT DECIDED HERE: `dim_team.team_country`, `dim_player.player_birth_country` and
  `dim_coach.coach_birth_country` carry 217/222/213 distinct values and very likely the same
  defect. Deliberately untouched - extrapolating across three dims from three known examples is
  what #69's DISCOVERY step exists to prevent.

2026-08-16 feat/72-onboard-mens-leagues — §11 blinded escalation, THREE questions, GitLab #72
(onboard 3 men's leagues, full coverage; evaluate 6 women's leagues, flagged weak; WSL not in the
provider catalog per the original issue research).

  PREMISE CHECK, before escalating: #72's research checked provider coverage at the SEASON level
  (the `/leagues` endpoint's `coverage.fixtures.*` booleans) for the CURRENT season only. That
  premise turned out to be materially incomplete for two of the three questions below — corrected
